import{_ as s,o,c as a,a0 as r}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"A job dies as a process group","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0036-a-job-dies-as-a-process-group.md","filePath":"adr/0036-a-job-dies-as-a-process-group.md","lastUpdated":1792074033000}'),t={name:"adr/0036-a-job-dies-as-a-process-group.md"};function n(i,e,d,p,c,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="a-job-dies-as-a-process-group" tabindex="-1">A job dies as a process group <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-job-dies-as-a-process-group" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A job dies as a process group&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Issue #139: operators saw processes of a <code>bash_bg</code> job (in their case, several <code>bun</code> processes) still running in <code>top</code> while no agent was working</p><ul><li>orphans that no session could list, wait on, or stop.</li></ul><p>Three mechanisms produced them. First, the wrapper shell could die while the job&#39;s processes were still alive (a supervisor killing the wrapper&#39;s pid, OOM): the descendants reparented to init, and the record declared the job exited because only the wrapper&#39;s liveness was checked and the exit code file - written by the dead wrapper - was absent. Second, the documented stop path signalled the pid <code>job_status</code> reported, which was the job&#39;s <code>sh</code> shell, not its process group: killing it left the command&#39;s own processes behind. Third, the stale cleanup deleted any job directory older than seven days, even a live one: a long-running job&#39;s directory mtime never advances (the wrapper writes only on exit), so the next <code>bash_bg</code> start removed the record and left the processes unmanageable.</p><p><strong>The job is one process group, and stopping it is a signal to that group.</strong> The wrapper is the group leader (<code>detached</code> makes the spawned shell the leader of a new group, in a new session), and the group id - the wrapper&#39;s pid - stays valid after the wrapper dies, as long as any member is alive. <code>job_status</code> and <code>job_wait</code> therefore report the group pid, and the stop path is <code>kill -- -&lt;pid&gt;</code> through the bash tool: one signal reaches every process of the job, wrapper dead or not, with no per-process bookkeeping.</p><p><strong>The record covers the job while any of its processes is alive.</strong> Liveness is the wrapper&#39;s pid normally, and the <code>child.pid</code> process once the wrapper is gone: a wrapper killed with SIGKILL cannot trap anything, but the command&#39;s shell survives it, so the record keeps saying running and the agent can wait on or group-stop the surviving tree. The stale cleanup skips any directory whose job is still alive, so a job that outlives the seven-day bound keeps its record instead of losing it.</p><p><strong>The shell scripts install no signal traps.</strong> The first fix attempt trapped HUP/INT/TERM/QUIT in the wrapper and the job&#39;s shell and cleared the group from inside the trap with <code>kill 0</code>. It failed in practice: dash and the bash versions that serve as <code>/bin/sh</code> disagree on trap semantics for non-interactive shells (signals ignored on entry cannot be re-trapped, and background children get INT/QUIT ignored), and bash 5.3 segfaulted on a <code>kill 0</code> group kill inside a trap while waiting on a child. Group management from the Node side - where the record is read and the pid is known - is uniform across the operator&#39;s Linux and macOS machines and needs no shell cooperation, including in the SIGKILL case where no trap could have fired at all.</p><p><strong>Rejected alternatives.</strong> Trapping and forwarding signals in the shell wrapper (above): unportable across dash and bash, and useless against SIGKILL. A Node watchdog process per job: a second long-lived process to manage, for a job the on-disk record already describes; the group kill needs no resident process. Stopping the job&#39;s processes when the agent process dies is a lifetime policy, decided separately in ADR 0037.</p>',8)])])}const u=s(t,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
